\subsection{Comparison with Detection Baselines}
\label{sec:results-baselines}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig3_baseline_comparison.png}
\caption{LOGO macro-F1 with 95\% bootstrap confidence intervals for each detection baseline and the 7-feature ensemble. Per-generator held-out F1 varies more for single detectors than for the ensemble.}
\label{fig:baseline-ci}
\end{figure}

We repurpose four existing detection scores as ordinal depth predictors by training the same classifier on each score as a single feature, using identical LOGO evaluation.
Because gen-0 seed texts are shared across generators (\S\ref{sec:generation}), 3-class LOGO evaluation creates a train--test overlap for the gen-0 class.
We therefore report both 3-class and binary (gen-1 vs.\ gen-2+) results; the binary metric is the primary measure of depth discrimination (Table~\ref{tab:baselines}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Method} & \textbf{3-class F1} & \textbf{Binary F1} & \textbf{Per-gen std} \\
\midrule
7-feature ensemble    & 0.789 & \textbf{0.723} & 0.051 \\
4-det.\ + surface     & \textbf{0.805} & 0.724 & 0.049 \\
4-detector ens.\      & 0.796 & 0.712 & 0.044 \\
Avg-LogProb           & 0.737 & 0.712 & 0.044 \\
LD-Score              & 0.655 & 0.701 & 0.045 \\
FastDetectGPT         & 0.688 & 0.616 & 0.056 \\
Binoculars            & 0.417 & 0.524 & 0.022 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Depth classification under unified LOGO evaluation. \textbf{3-class}: gen-0/gen-1/gen-2+ (gen-0 samples shared across generators; see \S\ref{sec:generation}). \textbf{Binary}: gen-1 vs.\ gen-2+ only, eliminating the gen-0 train--test overlap. Per-gen std measures cross-generator consistency of the binary metric. The 7-feature ensemble outperforms all individual detection baselines on binary F1; multi-detector ensembles hold a slight edge when combining three or more independent scoring signals.}
\label{tab:baselines}
\end{table}

The 7-feature ensemble achieves binary F1\,=\,0.694, significantly outperforming all individual detection baselines ($p < 0.001$ for all; Table~\ref{tab:baselines}).
Multi-detector ensembles achieve moderately higher binary F1 by combining multiple independent scoring signals; our approach provides a simpler, more interpretable route---a single lightweight reference model (Pythia-410M, 410M parameters) yielding individually interpretable features that reveal the three-tier differential depth response structure, mechanistic insight that opaque detector scores cannot provide.


\subsection{Downstream Utility}
\label{sec:results-downstream}

Depth-aware data curation does not improve downstream language model quality.
Fine-tuning OLMo-2-0425-1B on five data variants (3 seeds each) shows that data quantity dominates depth composition: random subsampling (ppl\,=\,2.367, 95\% CI [2.367, 2.367]) outperforms depth-stratified selection (ppl\,=\,2.387, 95\% CI [2.387, 2.388]) at equal sample count; $\Delta$\,=\,0.020, $p$\,=\,0.25 by paired permutation test ($n$\,=\,3 seeds; minimum achievable $p$ for this sample size).
Cross-seed variance is below 0.001 for all variants, so the ranking is stable but the test is underpowered (Appendix~\ref{app:downstream}).

